WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.260
When you look at this painting, what do you see?

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:08.060
A woman looking out a window?

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:11.840
How about now?

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:18.660
This famous painting by Salvador Dali is based on something called the “Lincoln illusion.”

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:24.980
The effect shows how blurring pixelated images can make it easier to recognize faces.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:28.720
Optical illusions are fun to look at.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:33.040
But, they can also tell us a lot about our sense of sight.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:36.900
So, how does the eye work exactly?

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:41.700
And is it really true that seeing is believing?

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:51.220
The human eye is pretty extraordinary.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.220
It has more than 2 million moving parts

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:58.140
and can move faster than any other muscle,

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:01.200
at less than 1/100th of a second.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:09.320
But when it comes to your vision, the way you perceive the world is actually thanks to your brain.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:14.840
First, light enters the eye through the pupil.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:22.840
The muscles behind the iris squeeze and stretch the lens to focus the light onto the back of the eye.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:27.280
This is the retina.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:35.480
The retina is covered with sensitive photoreceptors called rods, for dim light, and cones, for bright light.

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:43.120
The focused light hits the retina at a small pit called the fovea, which has the highest density of cone receptors.

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:49.180
We often compare our eyes to cameras,
but they actually work quite differently.

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:52.360
Our peripheral vision is very low resolution.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:57.460
We only see in full resolution at the
small point of our fovea.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:03.440
But, we barely notice it because our eyes are 
constantly refocusing on what we want to see,

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:06.320
like a high-resolution spotlight.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:14.500
Pixels, or picture elements, are the tiny, illuminated squares that make up a digital display.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:22.000
If two pixels are close to your eye, the retina sends two signals for the brain to interpret.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:26.040
Farther away, two pixels prompt one signal.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:31.440
That’s why a TV screen can have a 
lower pixel density than a smartphone,

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:35.640
but seen from farther away, it still appears seamless.

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:43.140
When stimulated, the rods and cones send signals to the optic nerve and back to the brain.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:50.080
Then, all of that visual information is processed to create the picture you see of the world.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:55.700
Unlike a camera, your brain can actually “fill in” missing information.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:03.220
For example, your eyes automatically blink every 3 to 4 seconds.

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:12.100
In fact, that means your eyes are technically closed for roughly 10% of your waking hours.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:20.260
Thankfully, your brain fills in those gaps, so you don’t feel like the lights are flickering on and off all day long.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:26.240
There’s a lot we still don’t understand about the complexities of our visual system.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:33.400
As technologies continue to innovate, the line between illusion and reality could start to blur.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:39.740
And when it comes to how we see the world, there might be more than meets the eye.

